2. Writing Your First Test
Let’s test a simple Counter component.
Component:
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Count: {count}&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setCount(c =&gt; c + 1)}&gt;Increment&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}
Test:
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
import Counter from './Counter';

test('increments count when button is clicked', async () =&gt; {
  render(&lt;Counter /&gt;);

  const button = screen.getByRole('button', { name: /increment/i });
  const countText = screen.getByText(/count: 0/i);

  expect(countText).toBeInTheDocument();

  // Simulate user click
  await userEvent.click(button);

  // Assert expectation
  expect(screen.getByText(/count: 1/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
});

Key Principle: Query by Role
Avoid testing implementation details (like class names or internal state). Instead, query by what the user sees: - getByRole('button', ...) - getByLabelText(...) - getByText(...)

3. Mocking API Calls
Don’t make real network requests in tests! Use MSW (Mock Service Worker) to intercept them.
// test-setup.ts
import { setupServer } from 'msw/node';
import { http, HttpResponse } from 'msw';

export const server = setupServer(
  http.get('/api/user', () =&gt; {
    return HttpResponse.json({ name: 'John Doe' });
  })
);

beforeAll(() =&gt; server.listen());
afterEach(() =&gt; server.resetHandlers());
afterAll(() =&gt; server.close());

Setting Up Workspaces
You don’t need complex tools like Nx or Turborepo to get started. Node.js understands workspaces natively.
package.json (Root):
{
  "name": "my-ai-studio",
  "private": true,
  "workspaces": [
    "apps/*",
    "packages/*"
  ]
}
Now, any folder inside apps or packages with its own package.json is treated as a workspace.

3. The Power of packages/shared
Imagine you have a TypeScript interface Project that is used by your Backend (Node/Rust), your Frontend (React), and your AI Service (Python).
Instead of copying types.ts into every folder, you create a shared package.
packages/shared/package.json:
{
  "name": "@my-studio/shared",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "main": "dist/index.js",
  "types": "dist/index.d.ts"
}
Usage in React App:
npm install @my-studio/shared
import { Project } from '@my-studio/shared';

export function ProjectCard({ project }: { project: Project }) {
  return &lt;div&gt;{project.name}&lt;/div&gt;;
}
Now, when you update the type definition in one place, every app gets the update instantly (and TypeScript will scream at you if you broke something).

Calling Rust from React
This is where the magic happens. You expose a Rust function as a “Command”, and call it from React like an async function.
1. Rust Side (main.rs):
#[tauri::command]
fn greet(name: &str) -&gt; String {
